\title{The CSS Dictionary, Homological Distance, and the Hypercube Code}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a precise three-way equivalence between the formulations of the
Calderbank--Shor--Steane (CSS) construction that are habitually used
interchangeably: an inclusion of nested classical linear codes
$C_2 \subseteq C_1$, the matrix orthogonality condition
$H_X H_Z^{\T} = 0$, and the isotropy (abelianness) of the generated Pauli
stabilizer group for the symplectic commutation form.  The equivalences are
identities of subspaces and groups, not numerical coincidences; the first two
hold over an arbitrary field, and no finiteness of the generating set is used
for the third.  Packaging a commuting pair as a length-two chain complex makes
the logical space the homology $C_1/C_2$ and yields
$k + \rank H_X + \rank H_Z = N$.

We then define the operational CSS distance --- the minimum weight of an
undetectable Pauli error that is not a stabilizer --- and prove that it equals
$\min(d_X,d_Z)$, the minimum of the systolic and cosystolic distances, under
the explicit hypothesis that logical operators of both types exist.  The key
structural input is that the CSS stabilizer group is the \emph{product}
$\rowsp(H_X)\times\rowsp(H_Z)$, so that stabilizer membership is componentwise.

We construct the $\FF_2$ incidence complex of the $n$-dimensional hypercube
$Q_n$, prove $\rank\partial_1 = 2^n-1$ (equivalently $\beta_0 = 1$) by a
Hamming-weight induction, and obtain the unconditional parameters
$[[\,n2^{n-1},\,2^{n-1}(n-2)+1,\,1\,]]$: the folk claim that the hypercube
homological code encodes one logical qubit holds only at $n=2$, and the
distance is $1$ despite the graph having girth $4$.  Finally we prove a rank
obstruction --- a graph incidence matrix satisfies $\rank M + 1 \le |V|$ ---
so a CSS code with independent $X$-checks, such as the Steane code, admits no
graph model.  The framework is certified end to end by rederiving $[[7,1,3]]$
from the Hamming check matrix.
\end{abstract}

